The corpse eating thing I don't agree with.  At worst, elves should receive 
a -1 alignment penalty or somesuch for eating an orc (orcs are extremely 
malicious; they'd probably delight in eating a hated foe).  But I do think 
the two races should receive minor penalties (to hit/defense) for using 
opposing racial equipment.

>The rest doesn't make any sense, either, come to that. Especially when
>one considers the vast differences between Nethack and Tolkien; in
>particular, the fact that in Nethack Elves are not the natural enemies
>of orcs (which really isn't true in the Lord of the Rings, either, as
>I recall), but rather simply another race on the same side of the
>lawful/chaotic battle. With the two races as allies (more or less),
>this all seems a little bit incoherent.

Tolkien's elves and orcs are long-time enemies, as best shown by things like 
Orcrist and Sting, elven weapons designed specifically for the purpose of 
fighting goblins and orcs.  The fact that both of those weapons exist in 
NetHack, I think, says something about the relationship between Tolkien's 
elves/orcs and NetHack's elves/orcs.

Also, I feel you're misinterpreting NetHack's alignment system somewhat. 
Elves and orcs may both be chaotic, but that certainly doesn't make them 
allies, in exactly the same way Asmodeus' being lawful does not make him an 
ally of the dwarves.  There's a lot of room in NetHack for co-aligned 
enmity.

